========================================================================================================
S2. THE FOUR CANDIDATE FRAMES -- and three of them die with a number
========================================================================================================
  FRAME C -- FULLY DRAGGED / CO-ROTATING (v_F = 0 on a circular orbit).
      Then u = Omega*v_F/a0 -> 0 and every bound circular orbit is in the DEEP-MOND limit.
      canonical rho_DE       |a|/g_bar at Earth = sqrt(a0/g) = 1.2562e-04  i.e. solar gravity WEAKER by 7961x
      alternate rho_total    |a|/g_bar at Earth = sqrt(a0/g) = 1.3802e-04  i.e. solar gravity WEAKER by 7245x
  [OK  ] FRAME C IS DEAD: a co-rotating frame makes solar gravity at Earth weaker by 7961x (canonical) / 7245x (alt)

  FRAME A -- COSMIC / CMB (v_F = speed relative to the CMB rest frame).
      Then a0_eff/a0 = v_orb/v_F, so each galaxy inherits its own peculiar velocity.
      Deep regime g_obs = sqrt(g_bar a0) => d log g_obs = 0.5 d log a0, so the a0 budget is
      2 * sigma_RAR. SPARC v_flat span and a field peculiar-velocity span:
      lambda = max(1, v_pec/v_orb) spans 1.00-30.00  => log10 spread = 1.477 dex in a0_eff
      alpha=1: budget = 2 x 0.1083 = 0.2166 dex ; over-budget factor = 6.82x
      alpha=2: budget = 2 x 0.1116 = 0.2232 dex ; over-budget factor = 6.62x
  [OK  ] FRAME A IS DEAD (banked result reproduced INDEPENDENTLY here): a cosmic-frame speed spreads a0_eff by 1.48 dex against a 0.223 dex budget = 6.6x over. The corpus's banked figure was 4.6x; mine is 6.6x, i.e. the banked one is the conservative end

  FRAME B -- BARYCENTRE-DRAGGED (u^mu co-moves with the BINARY barycentre, non-rotating).
      Gets the INTERNAL mode exactly right (v_F = v_int) but the barycentre's own galactic
      orbital mode is then referred to v_F = v_int, deflating its argument:
      canonical rho_DE       alpha=2: u_gal true 2.0436 -> eff 2.767e-03 (deflated 738.5x); galactic force boost 1.0953 -> 361.4; v_c would be 18.165x too high
      canonical rho_DE       alpha=1: u_gal true 2.0436 -> eff 2.767e-03 (deflated 738.5x); galactic force boost 1.2204 -> 361.4; v_c would be 17.208x too high
      alternate rho_total    alpha=2: u_gal true 1.6928 -> eff 2.292e-03 (deflated 738.5x); galactic force boost 1.1287 -> 436.3; v_c would be 19.661x too high
      alternate rho_total    alpha=1: u_gal true 1.6928 -> eff 2.292e-03 (deflated 738.5x); galactic force boost 1.2612 -> 436.3; v_c would be 18.599x too high
  [OK  ] FRAME B IS DEAD: a binary-barycentre-dragged frame deflates the GALACTIC mode's argument by 739x, boosting the barycentre's galactic acceleration so its circular speed is 18.16x that of a single star in the same potential (3996 vs 220 km/s). And in the exact DC limit (Omega->0) the argument -> 0 and the boost DIVERGES -- the prescription is not merely wrong but ill-defined
      (Reported both ways: FRAME B's INTERNAL prediction is nonetheless well defined and is
       exactly the ISOLATED two-body closure -- checked in S3 -- because a per-mode kernel
       has no cross term and therefore NO external-field effect at all.)

  FRAME G -- DOMINANT-MASS DRAGGED (u^mu co-moves with whichever mass dominates the local
      field). At a star in an isolated galaxy that is the galaxy, so v_F = v_orb and the RAR
      is reproduced BY CONSTRUCTION. At a wide binary the Galaxy dominates by 3.22x, so
      the frame is GALACTIC and the binary's internal mode is INFLATED. This is the only
      single-frame reading left standing, and the rest of this script is about its cost.
  [OK  ] at 10 kAU / 1 Msun the dominant-field rule selects the GALACTIC frame (a_gal/a_int = 3.225 > 1), i.e. FRAME G and not FRAME B -- the fork is decided by the ladder, not by choice

